{:nav-title "Negation"
 :content
 [:div
  [:h1 "Negation"]
  [:p "Sometimes you want to exclude results rather than include them. "
   "Datomic provides three tools for this: " [:code "not"] ", "
   [:code "not-join"] ", and " [:code "missing?"] "."]

  [:h2 "not"]
  [:p [:code "not"] " excludes any entity for which the inner pattern matches. "
   "Variables already bound in the outer query are automatically joined, but that's the only kind of "
   "variable a plain " [:code "not"] " can use. Every variable inside it must already be bound outside; "
   "there's no way to introduce a new one that stays local to the negation. "
   "Let's find Poison-type Pokemon that are not also Grass:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]
    [?e :pokemon/type "Poison"]
    (not [?e :pokemon/type "Grass"])]]
  [:p "Because " [:code ":pokemon/type"] " is " [:code ":db.cardinality/many"] ", this excludes Pokemon that have "
   [:em "any"] " Grass entry, not only pure Grass ones. Bulbasaur, Oddish, Bellsprout and every other "
   "Poison/Grass Pokemon are excluded, leaving only the Poison types that aren't also Grass. "
   "Try changing \"Poison\" to another type, or \"Grass\" in the " [:code "not"] " to \"Fire\" or \"Water\"."]

  [:h2 "not-join"]
  [:p "Here we want to find " [:strong "single-type Pokemon"] ": those for which there is no second, "
   "different type value. It reuses the " [:code "not="] " comparison from "
   [:a {:href "/cardinality-many"} "Cardinality many"] ". The check needs " [:code "?other"]
   ", a variable that only makes sense inside the negation. Restricting to speed above 100 "
   "keeps the result to a screenful:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name ?type
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]
    [?e :pokemon/type ?type]
    [?e :stat/speed ?speed]
    [(> ?speed 100)]
    (not-join [?e ?type]
              [?e :pokemon/type ?other]
              [(not= ?type ?other)])]]
  [:p "For each " [:code "?name"] "/" [:code "?type"] " pair, "
   [:code "not-join"] " checks whether a different type value " [:code "?other"] " exists on the same entity. "
   "If it does, the row is dropped. There are "
   [:ui/value "pokemon" [:find (count ?e) . :where [?e :pokemon/name ?name] [?e :pokemon/type ?type] (not-join [?e ?type] [?e :pokemon/type ?other] [(not= ?type ?other)])]]
   " single-type Pokemon in total; the speed filter above only shows the faster ones."]
  [:ui/try-tip
   [:p "Before running the next thing, predict: change " [:code "not-join [?e ?type]"] " above to plain "
    [:code "not"] " and run it. Datomic refuses: " [:code "?other not bound in not clause"]
    ". Every variable inside a plain " [:code "not"] " must already be bound outside it: "
    [:code "?other"] " is new, so there's nowhere for it to come from. "
    [:code "not-join"] " solves exactly this problem: it lets you introduce fresh variables inside the negation, as long "
    "as you declare explicitly which outer variables to join."]]

  [:h2 "missing?"]
  [:p [:code "missing?"] " checks that an entity has no value for a given attribute. "
   "Its arguments are the database binding " [:code "$"] ", the entity variable, and the attribute keyword."]
  [:p "In our Pokemon database, only a few have a " [:code ":pokemon/category"]
   ", with the value " [:code ":legendary"] ":"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/category :legendary]
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]]]
  [:p "In Datomic any entity can have any attribute, so for every other Pokemon that fact simply "
   "doesn't exist: they are " [:em "missing"] " the attribute. "
   "Where " [:code "get-else"] " in " [:a {:href "/function-clauses"} "Function clauses"]
   " kept those Pokemon and gave them a default, " [:code "missing?"] " keeps only them. "
   "Let's find non-legendary Pokemon with speed above 100, to keep the result to a screenful:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]
    [?e :stat/speed ?speed]
    [(> ?speed 100)]
    [(missing? $ ?e :pokemon/category)]]]
  [:p "There are "
   [:ui/value "pokemon" [:find (count ?e) . :where [?e :pokemon/name _] [(missing? $ ?e :pokemon/category)]]]
   " Pokemon in total that lack the " [:code ":pokemon/category"] " attribute; the speed filter above "
   "only shows the faster ones. " [:code "missing?"] " only filters; the rest of the query works normally."]
  [:ui/try-tip
   [:p "Change \"Poison\" and \"Grass\" in the " [:code "not"] " query at the top to another pair, "
    "like \"Water\" and \"Ice\". "
    "Or flip the " [:code "missing?"] " demo to find " [:em "legendary"] " Pokemon by replacing "
    [:code "missing?"] " with a data pattern " [:code "[?e :pokemon/category :legendary]"] "."]]]}
